00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.640
When most people try to get a new job. They&nbsp;
search for a listing. Then they apply. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:10.960
then they wait. Search… apply… wait… over&nbsp;
and over again. This is all wrong.
&nbsp;

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:15.840
I’ve hired over a hundred people in my career&nbsp;
and today I’m going to explain exactly how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:20.080
to get a new job in just 3 weeks. 
Hunting for a new job can feel daunting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:23.920
and believe me, I’ve been there, but I&nbsp;
promise you that there is a right way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:28.400
and a wrong way to go about finding a job.
The method I’m going to outline doesn’t just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:33.440
apply to finding a job. This works in sales,&nbsp;
operations, and even fundraising. I’ve actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:37.840
used this exact technique to raise over&nbsp;
$100 million dollars for my businesses.
&nbsp;

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:41.200
If you’re new here, I’m John. I live in&nbsp;
Los Angeles, California and have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:45.120
an entrepreneur for the past decade. Every&nbsp;
week I put out new videos about technology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:48.880
startups so please hit that subscribe button&nbsp;
and ring the bell so you don’t miss any.
&nbsp;

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:53.040
Okay, let me walk you through how most people&nbsp;
apply for jobs and then explain why it takes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:56.640
forever and rarely gets results. 
First, you start searching around for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:01.120
job postings, casually bouncing between websites&nbsp;
until you find something that looks interesting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:05.920
You read the post and see if you could qualify.&nbsp;
Usually the job description is extremely long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:09.760
and vague with dozens of qualifications&nbsp;
that sometimes contradict one another. 
&nbsp;

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:14.640
Then, after you find something that looks good,&nbsp;
you fill out an application and start to wait. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:19.360
you don’t hear back, you start to look for another&nbsp;
job listing, but this is extremely slow. 
&nbsp;

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:22.720
Then, even after you’ve applied,&nbsp;
you have to wait through several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:26.320
rounds of phone screens and interviews&nbsp;
to even find out if you got the job. 
&nbsp;

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:31.200
I see people all the time doing this and it takes&nbsp;
them forever to land a new job. This can be really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:36.320
dangerous, because, for better or worse, lots of&nbsp;
human resource departments are trained to filter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:41.760
out candidates with “resume gaps” - meaning that&nbsp;
more than a few months passed between jobs. 
&nbsp;

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:47.120
Following this process of search -&gt; apply -&gt;&nbsp;
interview is terrible and can lead to a downward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:52.240
spiral where it actually gets harder the longer&nbsp;
you work at it. But there’s a much better way.
&nbsp;

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:56.080
Essentially, instead of doing one&nbsp;
step after another, you want to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.080 --> 00:02:00.640
everything in parallel. That means instead&nbsp;
of just searching for a single job posting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:04.320
you need to collect hundreds of postings&nbsp;
before you fill out a single application. 
&nbsp;

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:09.440
Getting a job is just like selling a product,&nbsp;
it’s impossible to have a 100% conversion rate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:14.080
so you need to acknowledge that up front. Apple&nbsp;
has one of the best brands in the world, and some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:18.640
really great marketing to push their products,&nbsp;
but not every Apple advertisement is going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:23.680
lead to a customer purchase, so they spread their&nbsp;
bets out and market their products broadly.
&nbsp;

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:28.000
Let’s break the entire job hunting&nbsp;
process down into 3 key steps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:32.720
which could be done one week at a time.
First up is what I call research and development.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:38.000
This is what happens before you apply to a single&nbsp;
job posting. This whole process is about being as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:43.440
efficient as possible, so it’s important to do all&nbsp;
of your research in parallel. Your goal here is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:49.520
to build a massive list of prospects that you can&nbsp;
reach out to in step 2, so organization is key.
&nbsp;

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:54.080
Instead of searching for job postings in this&nbsp;
stage, you need to search for companies and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:57.760
more specifically, individual people&nbsp;
who have the authority to hire you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:02.480
Start by listing out every company that operates&nbsp;
in whatever industry you were just working in. 
&nbsp;

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:06.560
Then expand out based on your specific skill&nbsp;
set. So if you’ve been working in marketing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:11.600
at an electronics company, start by finding&nbsp;
as many electronics companies as possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:14.880
then broadening out to any company with&nbsp;
a growing marketing department. 
&nbsp;

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:18.640
Don’t try and filter too much at&nbsp;
this stage, instead just focus on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:23.040
gathering as much information as possible&nbsp;
and compiling everything in a spreadsheet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:27.440
If you’re used to using a tool like Notion&nbsp;
or Airtable, you can definitely use those,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:32.640
but Google Sheets is fine too. Just make sure to&nbsp;
note the companies name and how big they are. 
&nbsp;

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:37.040
Working for a small company can be dramatically&nbsp;
different than working for a large corporation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:40.880
so it’s worthwhile to keep size in&nbsp;
mind. You can usually find out how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:44.960
many employees a particular company has&nbsp;
by searching LinkedIn or just Google.
&nbsp;

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:49.760
Applying for jobs is really a sales process,&nbsp;
and not every lead is going to convert into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:54.480
job offer, so you want to maximize your&nbsp;
“top of funnel” leads by collecting as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:59.040
many potential opportunities as possible. 
It’s not crazy to compile a list of over a hundred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:03.760
potential companies before you start applying.&nbsp;
If you spend 5 minutes researching each company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:07.280
you should be able to compile a list&nbsp;
of 100 companies in just one day. 
&nbsp;

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:12.080
Once you have these companies listed out, start&nbsp;
developing your leads. This is where you start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:17.200
to find job listings and contact information&nbsp;
for managers who hire people with your skills.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:21.840
You should be adding information to each row in&nbsp;
your spreadsheet so you wind up with names and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:26.480
contact information for every target company. 
This is also a good time to start developing your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:30.640
personal elevator pitch. As you research&nbsp;
these companies and start seeing patterns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:34.840
in what they are hiring for, try to imagine&nbsp;
how you would fit into the organization. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:38.960
is often a big mismatch between the title&nbsp;
of the job and the description below. 
&nbsp;

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:42.560
At Google, a software engineer might be&nbsp;
expected to have a computer science degree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:48.480
and really understand algorithms, whereas a small&nbsp;
company outside the tech industry might still be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:53.760
hiring a software engineer, but just need someone&nbsp;
who can build a simple website. You want to figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:58.240
out what skills you have and what skills are&nbsp;
immediately adjacent to your experience. 
&nbsp;

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:02.880
If you feel like you’re lacking a specific skill,&nbsp;
now is a great opportunity to go and learn more.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:07.760
If you’ve been programming for a few years, but&nbsp;
don’t really have any experience with design,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:12.960
take time before your job search fully gets going&nbsp;
to try and get up to speed with the basics of that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:17.760
discipline. You can’t become an expert, but&nbsp;
it never hurts to be more well rounded.
&nbsp;

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:23.360
Then comes step 2: Parallel Outreach.
This is really the heart of this entire process.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:28.720
HR departments are notoriously slow to process&nbsp;
job applications and you never want to be in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:33.040
position where you’re waiting to hear back&nbsp;
about a job and have nothing to do. 
&nbsp;

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:37.920
In a great scenario, maybe 10% of job&nbsp;
applications are going to turn into interviews,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:41.840
so you really need to fill up your leads&nbsp;
spreadsheet before you start reaching out. 
&nbsp;

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:47.200
The goal is to only go through this process once,&nbsp;
and then just decide between multiple job offers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:52.000
at the end, but you can obviously repeat the&nbsp;
cycle if need be. The key is to keep the top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:57.520
of the funnel as big as possible so every&nbsp;
stage has the maximum chance of converting. 
&nbsp;

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:01.520
Applying to multiple jobs can be really&nbsp;
daunting and it’s not uncommon to think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:06.400
you should focus all your energy on a single&nbsp;
posting and spend an entire week preparing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:10.480
for that, but that’s a huge mistake. 
The first stage of any hiring process is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:14.880
basically just a filter. You might be the&nbsp;
perfect candidate for a position, and spend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:20.480
weeks preparing an amazing application, only&nbsp;
to find out that they hired someone a week ago&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:24.560
and just forgot to close out the job listing. 
So, you need to standardize your process for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:29.120
applying. You don’t want to spam people, but&nbsp;
a little bit of process optimization can go a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:34.240
long way here. After all, if you’re serious&nbsp;
about applying to a hundred jobs in one go,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:39.280
you can’t spend much time on each one. So, you&nbsp;
need to create a workflow for efficiency. 
&nbsp;

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:44.640
You can use a tool like MailMerge to automatically&nbsp;
fill out personalized emails, but sometimes this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:49.280
can backfire. Even if you add personal details&nbsp;
like the name of the person you’re emailing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:53.040
it can still feel like a marketing email,&nbsp;
where there were clearly just a few holes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:56.720
that got filled in automatically. 
Instead, you should have a few lines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.720 --> 00:07:01.440
of boilerplate about yourself, and then&nbsp;
customize the rest. In your spreadsheet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:05.120
you should have the name of the company and&nbsp;
the name of the person you’re reaching out to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:08.480
which is a good starting point. 
If you have time, be sure to add in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.480 --> 00:07:12.320
a few details about the position and why you&nbsp;
want to work for the company. This is a great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:16.800
place to show that you did some research and&nbsp;
mention a recent announcement by the company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:21.600
that you couldn’t have possibly autofilled. 
The key here is to stick to a schedule. If your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:25.760
target is to apply to a hundred companies&nbsp;
in a single week, you shouldn’t spend more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:29.760
than 25 minutes per company. That should&nbsp;
be plenty of time if you’re well organized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:34.160
and have all your supporting materials&nbsp;
like a resume and links to relevant work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:38.720
experience all together in one place. 
As you’re applying, really try and figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:42.480
out what steps you have to repeat over&nbsp;
and over again so you can automate them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:48.400
Tools like AutoHotKey and TextExpander can create&nbsp;
macros to keep you from typing the same sentence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:54.160
over and over again. Regardless of whether you use&nbsp;
Mac or PC, there are great options to help speed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:59.120
up your process and I’ll leave some links to&nbsp;
