&nbsp;

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:04.560
Step 3 is what I called targeted interviewing.&nbsp;
It’s easy to treat every interview the same way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:10.160
and put your maximum effort into one, but that’s&nbsp;
actually a common error. Interviewing for a new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:16.080
job should be as much about you deciding whether&nbsp;
or not you want the job as the company decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:19.840
whether or not they want to hire you. 
Job postings can be very misleading,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:24.160
and that’s not always the companies fault. The&nbsp;
hiring manager could be using an old template,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:27.600
or the company's goals could have shifted&nbsp;
since they put up the job req. 
&nbsp;

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:32.000
Either way, you can’t fall into the trap of&nbsp;
thinking you have to see every interview process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:37.200
to the end. Far too many managers would love to&nbsp;
hire someone who’s a bit overqualified, and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:41.040
don’t want to end up being that someone. 
It’s important to validate early on in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.040 --> 00:08:45.600
interview process exactly what you will be&nbsp;
doing and assess whether that’s a fit for you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:49.840
You might have done a particular job a few&nbsp;
years ago and hated it, so even though you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:54.480
technically qualified, you need to filter out any&nbsp;
companies that want you to do that old job. 
&nbsp;

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:57.440
There are two added benefits to&nbsp;
this more aggressive approach.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.440 --> 00:09:02.000
First, taking an active interest in the position&nbsp;
and really digging in to see if it’s what you want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:06.800
to be doing for the next few years shows a lot of&nbsp;
initiative, and that looks good in interviews. 
&nbsp;

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:12.080
Second, declining to advance to the next round&nbsp;
after a phone screen reveals that a job isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:17.280
quite right for you builds your confidence. If you&nbsp;
followed the first two steps properly, you should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:22.240
have plenty of interviews going on simultaneously&nbsp;
and never feel like you have to take a job because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:26.480
it’s the only opportunity available.
This strategy is all about parallelism and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:30.880
if you do it right, you’ll wind up in a great&nbsp;
situation where you can actually decide between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.880 --> 00:09:36.160
multiple job offers and weigh the pros and cons&nbsp;
of each. You might even be able to negotiate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:40.640
for a higher salary at the most interesting&nbsp;
company because you have some real leverage. 
&nbsp;

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:44.720
Even though human resources are legally limited&nbsp;
in what they can ask you about your job search,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.280 --> 00:09:49.680
most managers can sense when a&nbsp;
candidate they really want to hire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:55.200
has multiple offers, even if you don’t say it&nbsp;
explicitly. So use this to your advantage.
